[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Static Properties and Methods in PHP"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In PHP, static properties and methods belong to the class itself rather than to any specific instance of the class. This means you can access them without creating an object of the class. They are declared using the `static` keyword."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Static Properties"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Static properties are variables that are associated with a class rather than an instance of a class. They are useful for storing information that is shared across all instances of the class, or for storing global configuration data related to the class."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"<?php
class MyClass {
 public static $myStaticProperty = 'Hello, Static World!';

 public function __construct() {
 // You can access the static property from within an instance method
 echo self::$myStaticProperty . "\n";
 }

 public static function getStaticProperty() {
 return self::$myStaticProperty;
 }
}

// Accessing the static property directly using the class name
echo MyClass::$myStaticProperty . "\n";

// Creating an instance of the class (which also accesses the static property)
$obj = new MyClass();

// Accessing the static property through the static method
echo MyClass::getStaticProperty() . "\n";

?>"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, `$myStaticProperty` is a static property of the `MyClass` class. It can be accessed directly using the class name and the scope resolution operator (::), like this: `MyClass::$myStaticProperty`. It can also be accessed from within an instance method using `self::$myStaticProperty`."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Static Methods"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Static methods are functions that are associated with a class rather than an instance of a class. They are useful for creating utility functions that operate on the class itself, or for implementing the Singleton pattern."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"<?php
class MathUtils {
 public static function add($a, $b) {
 return $a + $b;
 }

 public static function subtract($a, $b) {
 return $a - $b;
 }
}

// Calling the static methods directly using the class name
echo MathUtils::add(5, 3) . "\n"; // Output: 8
echo MathUtils::subtract(10, 4) . "\n"; // Output: 6

?>"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, `add()` and `subtract()` are static methods of the `MathUtils` class. They can be called directly using the class name and the scope resolution operator (::), like this: `MathUtils::add(5, 3)`. Static methods cannot access instance properties or methods using `$this`, as they are not associated with any specific instance of the class."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Differences and Considerations"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Static members belong to the class, not the object."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Accessed using the class name and the scope resolution operator (::)."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Static methods cannot access instance properties or methods using `$this`."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Static properties are shared among all instances of the class."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Use Cases"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Storing configuration data that is shared across all instances of a class."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Creating utility functions that operate on the class itself."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Implementing the Singleton pattern (ensuring only one instance of a class is created)."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Creating counters or trackers that need to persist across multiple instances."}]
